ill house  having you and see somebody in the  uniform that looks like this hanging out  on the lawn just please don't tell  anyone don't call it but when I wasn't  loitering at the president's house in  his backyard I was spending really  valuable valuable times with friends and  with faculty here at Yale and the  University came to be much more than  just a place for education it came to be  a home for me and he was here that i  found a thoughtful community of students  and faculty members who not only taught  me about medicine and about life but who  believed in me at times when I doubted  myself when I was uncertain about the  future and it was these people who  helped me build a foundation for the  rest of my life and it is for that  reason that no matter where I go whether  it's through it was to residency  afterward or two jobs in DC or wherever  whatever may come in the future I will  always remain grateful to Yale for all  that it's given me the real power though  in beauty in this place is that each of  us has a different story to tell and  each of you is here for a reason that  reason isn't necessarily where you're  from it's not necessarily necessarily  the job that your parents do even if  they are in medicine but you're all here  because you know that you are capable of  accomplishing something big and you know  in your heart that big changes are  coming to our profession and that you  have a role to play in determining what  this change means for our patients when  I was a student at Yale I had absolutely  no idea that I would be Surgeon General  someday but what I did know is that I  was lucky to be here my father was  supposed to be a farmer in rural India  his father was a farmer who grew  coconuts Cameron and mangoes and rice my  father for years thought that he would  follow in his footsteps but somehow  through a mix of determination and luck  he became a doctor  and he made his he married my mother and  he made his way to the United States  when my family arrived here we didn't  have much in the way of resources or  money we didn't know many people in the  United States but my parents set up a  medical clinic where my father saw  patients where my mother ran the  operation and together over time they  built these extraordinary relationships  with patients the ones that ultimately  inspired me to go into medicine myself  now periodically my family would reflect  on our journey and we would reflect on  how grateful and lucky we were to live  in a country where there are so many  doors of opportunity that were open to  us and we've known though that this  hasn't always been the case the doors of  opportunity haven't always been open to  everyone in America in fact only a few  decades ago somebody like me may not  have been welcome at a place like you  much less in the office of the Surgeon  General and while we know that as a  country we still have a lot of work to  do there are more and more voices in  this country thankfully that are  fighting for equality and opportunity in  medicine and that is a very good thing  but the big reason that I'm here today  is to tell you that we have a unique  opportunity at this point in history to  usher in the next Golden Age of medicine  an age where good health is within the  reach of every person an age where  physicians are moral leaders who  alleviate suffering not only by healing  the sick but also by working to prevent  illness in their clinics and in their  communities our country desperately  needs this but whether or not we create  and maintain this golden age in medicine  will depend on what you the current and  future leaders in medicine do and what  do I mean by that well we know that  medicine cannot stand alone as doctors  and as medical professionals we have a  unique responsibility to not only care  for individuals but to do something that  has been  part of our legacy for millennia and  that's just safeguard the health of our  community as well but over 