ighting over what those  values of mint and who they apply to has  in fact been at the center of our worst  moments as well but today we live in  this unique moment in history a moment  where the combination of technology  knowledge and the breaking down of  barriers have made it possible for more  people than ever before to make a  difference in their community and that  is why I believe that together today we  can create a golden age of Medicine we  can only do this if we have leaders who  are bold only if we have leaders who can  articulate a clear vision for help for  our country in choosing the path of  medicine all of us have chosen to become  those leaders and the current and the  country urgently needs you to be those  leaders I hear about this urgency every  day when i visit communities across the  country is Surgeon General I hear from  mayors and from doctors about how the  prescription opiate epidemic is  destroying small towns and big cities  alike responsible for nearly sixteen  thousand deaths a year in fact I hear  from too many parents that addiction and  mental illness are still seen as moral  failings in our country instead of as  what they are chronic illnesses they  need to be treated with care and  compassion but most concerning to me is  the sense i get from too many people  that they have lost faith in their  ability to improve their own health more  and more people feel that the forces  that determine their health are beyond  their control they feel that their  health is increasingly in the hands of  the food industry of insurance companies  and of others who have money and clout  but there are also reasons to be hopeful  and each of us here today can show  people that they do in fact have the  power to change your health to change  your communities and to change their  country and I draw inspiration from  stories that I hear on the road as well  stories like the one I heard earlier  this week when I was in Las Vegas and  when I met young parents who every day  walk their kids to school 1.7 miles each  way and they do this because they want  their kids to be healthy I also draw  inspiration from programs like the one I  came across in Roanoke Virginia where  the community has come together to teach  children and adults about how to prepare  healthy foods with fruits and vegetables  and they've been able to document that  they are changing the preference that  kids have around food as a result I also  draw inspiration from Indiana where I  visited just a few days ago and where I  found that law enforcement educators and  public health leaders are in fact coming  together to take on the opiate epidemic  which is ravaged Indiana they are  inspiring stories of success like this  that are dotted all across our country  but the challenge before us is that we  have to make sure that these stories are  the norm not the exception that is our  responsibility and I say our  responsibility deliberately because it's  not just your responsibility but it's  mine is as well last week I joined with  a number of nonprofit organizations and  businesses to launch a call to action  around walking and walkable communities  now I know what you might be thinking  walking how boring how could that be  relevant to help but it turns out  walking is in fact quite exciting  because  because it turns out that walking might  be one of the most powerful tools that  we have to roll back the tide of chronic  illness that plagues our country  something as small as walking it turns  out can have a big impact also on  helping us to reclaim a culture of  physical activity in America something  that has disappeared all too often from  our schools and from our workplaces now  everyone in this room may understand  that but think about the people outside  this room at a time when three out of  ten Americans live in communities that  don't have adequate sidewalks at a time  of chronic illnesses like diabetes and  heart disease and cancer are re