y  who brings groups together to play their  part in building a culture of prevention  and is a medical community we also need  to start valuing that kind of community  work as much as we value traditional  metrics like publishing because ultimate  success isn't just about treatment it  means ensuring that our patients never  have to walk through the door of our  office or a hospital in the first place  I want us to be a nation that is as good  at preventing disease as we are at  treating it that is the kind of success  that we are after in the end we face big  challenges as a country that's  undeniable we hear about these  challenges every day when we read the  newspaper or watch the news but never  ever believe that these challenges are  bigger than our collective will because  they are not and if you ever doubt this  remember that it was collective will  that allowed a small band of colonists  to take on the mighty British Empire and  win it was collective will that allowed  our nation to face down unspeakable  violence and racism and passed the  landmark civil rights act and it was  collective will that 30 years ago  allowed us to take on one of the  greatest challenges of our time HIV  this is an epidemic that was surrounded  by stigma by fear and by uncertainty  both in the general public and even in  the medical profession I'm sure many of  you may be familiar with the Ryan White  store Ryan was a young man from Indiana  who struggled with hemophilia and he was  infected with HIV through a blood  transfusion and Ryan face it incredible  prejudice and even hate from those who  were around him and yet when he was  presented with the opportunity to lead  he took that opportunity and he along  with his mother Jeanne made it their  life's work to change hearts and minds  and today thanks to that work thanks to  their leadership over 530,000 Americans  received treatment through the wine  white hiv/aids program and although we  have made great progress in our fight