ink including members of  Congress so the onus often is on us to  speak up to share our views and to do  that through meetings your public  discourse through writing letters to the  editor and offense the more we do that  the more I believe we can change how our  country looks at addiction way in the  back so you mentioned a large number of  deaths from okla opioids but you didn't  mention twice as many deaths from  something else which is gun violence and  I wonder in this era where doctors often  can't ask if there's a gun at home as it  secured safely what the opportunities  for bringing down that number are and I  knows that a politically difficult issue  since the puppet came up in connection  with your own confirmation well thank  you for that question and gun violence  is an important public health issue I've  said that before I've said that since my  confirmation and I'll say it again  because it's the truth and all of us  know it the challenges of gun violence  are complex they're not just about laws  they're also about mental health they're  also about how we fundamentally treat  each other and about the relationships  that we build with each other but one  thing we know for certain is that to  address any of those issues we need to  be able to have a dialogue about the  topic and too often the issue of gun  violence gets politicized one of the  things that doctors can do in a very  powerful way is to help take issues like  gun violence out of a political lens and  to put it back into a human context by  telling the stories of people who are  impacted by  it including patients their families and  their providers this is not just  theoretical I've actually seen doctors  do this I've seen doctors write letters  about the patients they've cared for who  are victims of gun violence I've seen  them come and meet with lawmakers to  share their experiences and their ideas  for what has to change I've seen them  speak at public rallies and move public  opinion and