nd connect  them with institutions and individuals  who are in need that shift from being  individual providers of care to also  being leader  who can communicate and who can convene  that's a very important shift for us to  make it is a cultural shift to be very  clear and it's one that I'm hopeful that  we can achieve quickly because truly the  health of our communities depends on it  thank you I was wondering how do you  think we can better negotiate the role  of government in aiding Americans and  becoming agents in their own healthcare  so for example i'm thinking of the you  know the recent controversy of the past  couple years of the soda restrictions in  new york city you know so how can  government play a better role in you  know making Americans agents of their  own health care rather than being  completely prescriptive it's a good  question and one that I reference a bit  in my remarks because one of my great  concerns is that people have lost that  sense of control in their lives they  feel like they don't have the ability to  determine whether they're healthy or not  and to be clear there are forces outside  that are very influential right there  are some people who live in  neighborhoods where grocery stores are  not available there's some folks who  live in areas where buying fresh fruits  and vegetables is just prohibitively  expensive these are real problems but a  focus on those problems alone obscures  the fact that we have choice and we  often have more choice than we think  they use the example you raise of what  New York attempted around soda policies  back during a mayor bloomberg's  administration while many of those  policies that they attempted didn't  actually pass guess what happened to  sort of consumption rates in New York  City by virtue of the campaign's that  they ran to raise awareness about the  issue soda consumption actually dropped  significantly so does just one example  whether it's soda or whether it's  choices that people ma