n my family arrived here we didn't  have much in the way of resources or  money we didn't know many people in the  United States but my parents set up a  medical clinic where my father saw  patients where my mother ran the  operation and together over time they  built these extraordinary relationships  with patients the ones that ultimately  inspired me to go into medicine myself  now periodically my family would reflect  on our journey and we would reflect on  how grateful and lucky we were to live  in a country where there are so many  doors of opportunity that were open to  us and we've known though that this  hasn't always been the case the doors of  opportunity haven't always been open to  everyone in America in fact only a few  decades ago somebody like me may not  have been welcome at a place like you  much less in the office of the Surgeon  General and while we know that as a  country we still have a lot of work to  do there are more and more voices in  this country thankfully that are  fighting for equality and opportunity in  medicine and that is a very good thing  but the big reason that I'm here today  is to tell you that we have a unique  opportunity at this point in history to  usher in the next Golden Age of medicine  an age where good health is within the  reach of every person an age where  physicians are moral leaders who  alleviate suffering not only by healing  the sick but also by working to prevent  illness in their clinics and in their  communities our country desperately  needs this but whether or not we create  and maintain this golden age in medicine  will depend on what you the current and  future leaders in medicine do and what  do I mean by that well we know that  medicine cannot stand alone as doctors  and as medical professionals we have a  unique responsibility to not only care  for individuals but to do something that  has been  part of our legacy for millennia and  that's just safeguard the health of our  community as well but over 